- What is mathematics (blog post, August 2026) (source: source.txt)
    Summary: As AI automates proof and verification, mathematics is shifting toward deciding which patterns matter, a partly social judgment with no established home. (¶14, ¶84, ¶85, ¶86)
        - 1 The old definition stopped working: proof production and verification are being automated, while choosing what to prove stayed human. (¶6, ¶7, ¶10, ¶14, ¶15)
            - 1.1 A professor's post: research now feels like grading a clever student desperate for an A who does not mind lying. (¶1, ¶2) [reported]
            - 1.2 The old definition: mathematics is the discipline of finding patterns where claims can be proven. (¶3)
                - 1.2.1 Nuance: Under that definition mathematics was the production of correct statements, though mathematicians built intuition and found clues to patterns. (¶10)
            - 1.3 Experts could settle whether an idea was right, most of the time, which set math apart from fields with opinions, even physics. (¶4)
            - 1.4 In summer 2026 Tao's ICM lecture and Moradifam's essay asked what mathematics is once proof production stops being scarce. (¶5) [reported]
                - 1.4.1 Nuance: The author mostly agrees, but they stop where he must speculate; he wants to push past that edge. (¶5)
            - 1.5 First Proof's late May 2026 test: four leading systems solved seven of ten unpublished research problems to publication quality. (¶7, ¶8, ¶94)
                - 1.5.1 Evidence: Compute cost ten to a thousand dollars per problem. (¶8, ¶94)
                - 1.5.2 The one step they could not automate was choosing the problems, the judgment this post is about. (¶8, ¶94)
                - 1.5.3 Evidence: Method: collect unpublished problems across fields, pick ten per batch, give one attempt each, grade blind with human referees. (¶93, ¶94)
            - 1.6 Evidence: Early 2026: an AI system produced a machine-checked Lean proof of Erdős Problem #728. (¶9)
                - 1.6.1 Evidence: The system combined OpenAI's GPT-5.2 Pro with Harmonic's Aristotle. (¶95)
                - 1.6.2 Evidence: Nat Sothanaphan translated it into a conventional argument, and the problem is now marked proved on the Erdős problems list. (¶95)
            - 1.7 Evidence: May 2026: an OpenAI reasoning model disproved the planar unit distance conjecture Erdős posed in 1946. (¶9)
                - 1.7.1 Evidence: The conjecture said the maximum number of unit distances among $n$ points in the plane grows like $n^{1+o(1)}$. (¶95)
                - 1.7.2 Evidence: The model built infinite families of point sets with at least $n^{1+\varepsilon}$ unit distances, using algebraic number theory. (¶95)
                - 1.7.3 Evidence: A paper with Alon, Bloom, Gowers and others digested the result for the community. (¶95)
            - 1.8 The split: the model produced the result, while humans checked, digested, and presented it. (¶9, ¶10)
            - 1.9 The production of proofs has been automated, or it will be, under Tao's working assumption. (¶10)
            - 1.10 Evidence: Seven of ten novel problems solved to publication quality for a few hundred to a few thousand dollars of compute. (¶11)
            - 1.11 What was not automated: people still chose the problems and handed them to the models one at a time. (¶11)
                - 1.11.1 Nuance: The models still might need a lot of handholding. (¶11)
                - 1.11.2 Conjecture generators propose patterns, but they do not decide which ones matter. (¶11)
            - 1.12 So either mathematics is over, or the old definition was wrong. (¶11)
                - 1.12.1 Rebuttal: It is not over: the author thinks it has just started, and wants to explore how the definition was wrong. (¶12, ¶13)
                - 1.12.2 Nuance: Maybe the proving was the chore and the finding was always the real thing. (¶13)
                - 1.12.3 Nuance: The author assumes mathematics is what we humans do, like a religion assuming its god. (¶13)
            - 1.13 The proof process has a second half: verification, often implicitly part of it, and that is being automated too. (¶14, ¶15)
                - 1.13.1 Tao's Working Hypothesis covers verification as a mathematical task; the open question is what level of error we tolerate. (¶16)
                - 1.13.2 Evidence: The calibration story: Vladimir Voevodsky had to retract foundational work years later. (¶17)
                    - 1.13.2.1 Evidence: Voevodsky won the Fields Medal in 2002. (¶17)
                    - 1.13.2.2 Evidence: In the late nineties Pierre Deligne went through the foundational paper line by line and found a key lemma's proof wrong, unsalvageable as stated. (¶17)
                    - 1.13.2.3 Evidence: The earlier Kapranov paper on ∞-groupoids had a main theorem eventually shown false, which Voevodsky accepted only years later. (¶17)
                    - 1.13.2.4 Evidence: Multiple groups studied, used, and taught those papers; nobody noticed for years. (¶17)
                    - 1.13.2.5 Evidence: Voevodsky asked who would ensure he forgot nothing and made no mistake, if mistakes in simpler arguments take years to uncover. (¶18)
                    - 1.13.2.6 Evidence: He spent the rest of his career pushing computer proof verification because he no longer trusted the human process. (¶18)
                - 1.13.3 Human verification is not reliable: a Fields medalist's foundational paper carried a wrong key lemma for years and an unchallenged false theorem for decades. (¶19)
                    - 1.13.3.1 AI verification with a comparable error rate will not look unacceptable; it will look like business as usual. (¶19)
                    - 1.13.3.2 Premise: generation and verification are both automated and both imperfect, roughly as imperfect as the people they replace. (¶19)
                    - 1.13.3.3 Nuance: The AI-side error rate is assumed, not measured. (¶19)
                    - 1.13.3.4 Evidence: The volume compounds: years to surface a mistake by hand, a million checks a day by machine. (¶19)
        - 2 Mathematics was never theorem production: it was a process of thinking, and the process was the product. (¶20, ¶21)
            - 2.1 The old definition was wrong, and both authors know it. (¶21)
            - 2.2 Moradifam: we teach math for the struggle, not the facts; the student discovers what matters and what does not. (¶22) [reported]
                - 2.2.1 That process builds the habit of tracking assumptions, moving between examples and principles, and the confidence to keep thinking. (¶22)
            - 2.3 Cognitive offloading delegates mental effort to a tool; the illusion of explanatory depth confuses fluency with ownership. (¶23)
                - 2.3.1 A student who can summon a complete, fluent solution within seconds risks equating exposure to an idea with understanding it. (¶23)
                - 2.3.2 Nuance: A calculator does not undermine arithmetic for a student who already understands place value; it becomes harmful when it arrives before the understanding. (¶23)
                - 2.3.3 AI adds a twist: it produces plausible explanations in a confident tone, so student and instructor can mistake output for learning. (¶23)
            - 2.4 Example: An airport ad from an early AI company: let the AI do the thinking for you. (¶24) [reported]
                - 2.4.1 The ad treats thinking the way earlier automation treated laundry, as toil to be eliminated. (¶24)
                - 2.4.2 Fixing a car is instrumental; thinking is constitutive, since working through a problem is what builds judgment. (¶24)
                - 2.4.3 Offload the chore and you keep the car; offload the thinking and you keep an answer with an unchanged mind. (¶24)
            - 2.5 Nuance: The boundary is blurry: the same was said about mental arithmetic, memorizing poetry, and navigation. (¶25)
                - 2.5.1 Nuance: Some of those losses turned out fine; some cost something real. (¶25)
                - 2.5.2 Evidence: Longitudinal work: more GPS use predicts a steeper decline in hippocampal-dependent spatial memory (Dahmani & Bohbot, 2020). (¶25)
                - 2.5.3 Evidence: Long-term GPS use is associated with worse mental rotation and perspective-taking, which hurts environmental learning (Ruginski et al., 2019). (¶25)
            - 2.6 The question is which cognitive activities are load-bearing for everything else. (¶26)
                - 2.6.1 Calculation turned out not to be. (¶26)
                - 2.6.2 Problem framing, noticing what is missing, deciding what is worth attention look load-bearing, precisely what the ad proposes to outsource. (¶26)
                - 2.6.3 Proving is the gym where the finding is trained: the slog of a rigorous proof builds the muscle for spotting patterns. (¶26)
                - 2.6.4 The chore and the training are the same act; even failed proofs bring a slew of ideas and learning. (¶26)
            - 2.7 Tao reaches the same place through exposition: AI-polished proofs are close to flawless in spelling, grammar, and formatting, almost too flawless. (¶27) [reported]
                - 2.7.1 They dwell on trivialities, rush past the novel parts, and fail to connect to prior literature. (¶27)
                - 2.7.2 They remove friction: in human proofs the difficult parts keep a natural friction that makes the reader slow down. (¶28)
                - 2.7.3 An over-polished proof removes natural and artificial friction; the reader sails through without learning the key ideas. (¶28)
                - 2.7.4 Nuance: Paradoxically, mistakes in human exposition can be helpful: they mark where the work is. (¶28)
                - 2.7.5 Thurston: success is whether what we do enables people to understand and think more clearly and effectively about math, not production quotas. (¶28) [reported]
            - 2.8 The struggle, the friction, the process point at the same thing, the thing the pipeline optimizes away. (¶29)
                - 2.8.1 Nuance: The slides are a delight to go through and completely non-technical. (¶29)
        - 3 Tao's pipeline: proof abundance threatens to break the correlation of the community's goals, and the bottleneck shifts to digestion, the work treated as low prestige. (¶30, ¶31, ¶37, ¶38)
            - 3.1 Tao keeps going where neither document does: once the struggle stops being scarce, what is the community optimizing for? (¶30)
            - 3.2 Working Hypothesis: AI tools will reasonably soon perform a reasonable fraction of research-level mathematics, with reasonable success, quality, supervision, and cost. (¶32) [reported]
                - 3.2.1 Tao asks you not to believe it, only assume it, then asks which goals and values the community actually pursues, explicit and implicit. (¶32)
                - 3.2.2 Nuance: That question was historically delegated to the humanities. (¶32)
            - 3.3 Goodhart's law: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure. (¶33)
                - 3.3.1 The community's goals were positively correlated: solving problems, building theory, training the next generation, creating enduring aesthetic works. (¶33)
                - 3.3.2 Any one or two goals could proxy for the rest, and the rest could stay implicit. (¶33)
                - 3.3.3 AI optimization threatens to break the correlation; generative AI is ungrounded by nature and AI companies have financial incentives. (¶33)
                - 3.3.4 So AI use is particularly vulnerable to Goodhart, and the goals can diverge. (¶33)
            - 3.4 Case study: start with the goal solve as many unsolved problems as possible. (¶34)
                - 3.4.1 Refine once: add verification, or you get a pile of incorrect solutions. (¶34)
                - 3.4.2 Refine again: add exposition, because correct but unreadable solutions are not progress either. (¶34)
                - 3.4.3 Evidence: On erdosproblems.com, dozens of AI-generated proofs, many likely correct, await an expert volunteer to verify and vouch for them. (¶34)
                - 3.4.4 Evidence: In several cases the human submitters declared themselves unqualified to verify. (¶34)
            - 3.5 Refine again: add community acceptance, since a proof no one has digested contributes nothing to the field. (¶35)
                - 3.5.1 Acceptance is slow, human, and external; authors and their AI tools cannot optimize it directly. (¶35)
                - 3.5.2 Volunteer editors and referees produce it, work less prestigious than generating proofs, yet it turns individual achievements into collective progress. (¶35)
            - 3.6 Refine once more: add canonicalization, incorporation into the definitive theory, textbooks, and reference material taught to the next generation. (¶35)
                - 3.6.1 This is the slowest stage, the least amenable to optimization, and the most valuable. (¶35)
            - 3.7 Example: Tao's pipeline runs open problems through generation, verification, publication, exposition, digestion, and canonicalization into definitive solutions. (¶36) [reported]
            - 3.8 Punchline: mathematics is moving from proof scarcity to proof abundance. (¶37)
                - 3.8.1 Under the Working Hypothesis, mismatches appear at every stage: AI proofs accumulate unverified, verified proofs await readable writeups. (¶37)
                - 3.8.2 Correct and well-written proofs overwhelm peer review; published proofs are too numerous to ever digest. (¶38)
                - 3.8.3 The bottleneck shifts to proof digestion, the work currently treated as low prestige. (¶38)
            - 3.9 Evidence: Paul Halmos, a top mathematician, wanted to be known for exposition rather than cutting-edge research. (¶38)
            - 3.10 Tao's recommendations: normalize responsible disclosure of AI assistance, decrease the emphasis on generation and on being first. (¶38) [reported]
                - 3.10.1 Increase the emphasis on exposition, publication, and canonicalization. (¶38)
                - 3.10.2 Evidence: He points to the Leiden Declaration, a community statement on AI in mathematics endorsed by the International Mathematical Union. (¶38)
                - 3.10.3 Rule of thumb: if authors cannot convincingly give a clear, expert-level talk on their correct, properly attributed result, do not publish it. (¶38)
            - 3.11 The LinkedIn professor is the human version: his reward for succeeding is demotion to checking others' work. (¶39)
                - 3.11.1 Evidence: Experimental science saw the same inversion when measurement got cheap: prestige moved from measuring to designing experiments. (¶39)
                - 3.11.2 That prestige inversion is already happening. (¶39)
        - 4 Moradifam: as routine tasks get automated, problem framing, judgment, and taste become more visible and more valuable. (¶40, ¶43)
            - 4.1 Example: The Davies results used machine learning as an intuition pump, finding correlations in knot theory that humans then formalized. (¶41)
            - 4.2 Example: AlphaTensor framed matrix multiplication search as a game and found algorithms humans had not. (¶41)
            - 4.3 The pattern repeats: AI proposes, humans check, digest, and present. (¶41)
                - 4.3.1 Then the line of work speeds up into the 2026 results, where AI takes on most of the discovery process. (¶41)
            - 4.4 Mathematics already accepts opaque results: the four-color theorem and the classification of finite simple groups. (¶42)
                - 4.4.1 We use them because they survived extensive communal verification, though no single person holds the whole proof in mind. (¶42)
                - 4.4.2 As AI produces longer and more complex proofs, we may accept truths whose full deductive structure lies beyond any individual. (¶42)
            - 4.5 Understanding was historically constructive: you deepen it by reconstructing the proof. (¶43)
                - 4.5.1 When a result arrives from an opaque process, understanding shifts from constructive to hermeneutic. (¶43)
                - 4.5.2 The mathematician becomes less a builder and more an interpreter, extracting meaning from a proof that cannot explain itself. (¶43)
                - 4.5.3 Nuance: The author thinks Moradifam's thesis is right as far as it goes. (¶43)
        - 5 Both documents leave importance on stable human ground, and the author argues importance was always a partly social judgment. (¶44, ¶45, ¶46, ¶49)
            - 5.1 Moradifam: what the community regards as interesting, deep, or worth understanding remains human, shaped by taste, experience, and collective culture. (¶45) [reported]
            - 5.2 Tao's equivalent: community acceptance is ultimately external and cannot be optimized purely by authors and their AI tools. (¶45) [reported]
            - 5.3 Both locate the judgment of importance in a stable human ground, and neither looks underneath it. (¶45)
                - 5.3.1 Nuance: The author's genre is a blog post: building on their ground, not correcting it; the next step is speculation, not scholarship. (¶45)
            - 5.4 The two threads meet: if the process of thinking is the product, what matters most is what the math was worth thinking about. (¶46)
                - 5.4.1 The author suspects importance was never a stable, purely human judgment. (¶46)
            - 5.5 The first lever of importance was applicability: did a problem solve something real in science, engineering, or society? (¶47)
                - 5.5.1 It was measurable fairly directly and partly driven by where research funding lived: money went where results were wanted. (¶47)
            - 5.6 The second lever was the taste of the big names: what the field's influencers thought was important. (¶48)
                - 5.6.1 That taste was a compound: partly fame, partly genuine interest, partly network favoritism. (¶48)
                - 5.6.2 Evidence: Nash, allegedly but not verified, was infamous for working only on problems that could get him famous. (¶48)
                - 5.6.3 Network favoritism: I know you, so your problem interests me, we both work on it, and both grow in social position. (¶48)
            - 5.7 Neither lever is pure mathematical judgment; both are social. (¶49)
                - 5.7.1 The field could pretend otherwise while the process was slow, local, and invisible; the AI era removes the cover story. (¶49)
            - 5.8 The author owns the irony: he leans on Tao and Moradifam, exactly the kind of authority he says to interrogate. (¶50)
                - 5.8.1 Nuance: He leans on them because their reasoning is checkable, not because their names are big. (¶50)
                - 5.8.2 Nuance: He is trying to show his work and is not sure he fully lives up to it. (¶50)
        - 6 The influencer economy replaces standing earned over decades of checkable work with engagement, and the canon becomes whatever holds attention. (¶51, ¶52, ¶59, ¶61)
            - 6.1 Everything is sales: you work more to sell what you create than to make it. (¶52)
                - 6.1.1 Partly globalization: competition used to be across town, now it is everyone with an internet connection. (¶52)
                - 6.1.2 Production got cheap everywhere, so distribution became the constraint. (¶52)
            - 6.2 The same is about to happen to mathematics: generating and verifying become cheap, while attention stays scarce. (¶52)
                - 6.2.1 Markets for scarce attention develop influencer structure. (¶52)
            - 6.3 Example: The internet did this to writing: blogs in vastly greater volume, fewer books read, important topics deprioritized relative to popular ones. (¶53)
            - 6.4 Example: Social media pushed it further: toward video and voice, short-form clips and podcasts everywhere. (¶54)
                - 6.4.1 People now listen to random dudes who may not be qualified rather than read a book by an expert. (¶54)
                - 6.4.2 More interest in food supplements than world affairs: paid podcasters make topics easier to digest, more relatable, and urgent for your life. (¶54)
                - 6.4.3 It often supports your current understanding rather than challenging it. (¶54)
            - 6.5 Something similar will happen to mathematics; timing is unsure, it might take years, probably decades, not months. (¶55)
                - 6.5.1 The current influencers are still highly qualified: Tao, Gowers, and slightly lower ranked mathematicians with successful social media presence. (¶55)
                - 6.5.2 They earned their standing when the canon was set slowly, by journals and seminars. (¶55)
            - 6.6 The old gatekeeping mechanism was not good, but what is coming is definitely worse. (¶56)
                - 6.6.1 Evidence: Levitsky, in How Democracies Die, talks nostalgically about old gatekeeping in politics; the author is not saying that. (¶56)
                - 6.6.2 Gatekeeping has a function and needs to be more merit based, but the field is moving the opposite direction. (¶56)
            - 6.7 The problem is not the current influencers; it is the mechanism that selects the next generation. (¶56, ¶57)
            - 6.8 When compute becomes the budget, a research problem is worth what it costs in compute time. (¶57)
                - 6.8.1 Allocation will follow direct impact on other fields first and what the influencers of mathematics advertise second. (¶57)
                - 6.8.2 A long tail of problems would no longer flow to theory from applications, which solve them themselves. (¶57)
                - 6.8.3 Physics once developed the math it needed; now problems get solved internally, with each field's own compute budget. (¶57)
            - 6.9 A naive faith treats figures like Tao as disinterested, as if canonization flowed from pure mathematical judgment. (¶58)
                - 6.9.1 The assumption is subtler than corruption: that their attention allocation is a public good, when it is a private act with public consequences. (¶58)
                - 6.9.2 A Tao blog post can redirect years of collective effort, and nobody audits whether the redirection was optimal. (¶58)
                - 6.9.3 The prestige itself becomes the justification; so far private acts likely align with the public interest, but an influencer economy guarantees nothing. (¶58)
            - 6.10 Historically the faith sort of worked for structural reasons that are eroding, not because mathematicians are unusually virtuous. (¶59)
                - 6.10.1 Old gatekeepers were trustable structurally: standing built over decades of checkable work, judgments open to community checking, wrong calls costly. (¶59)
                - 6.10.2 The influencer economy replaces that with standing earned by engagement, answerable to nothing but more engagement. (¶59)
                - 6.10.3 Old gatekeepers were slow influencers in a low-velocity, low-visibility attention economy of journals, seminars, and letters. (¶59)
                - 6.10.4 Attention did not convert to much, so there was little to gain from attention-maximizing behavior. (¶59)
                - 6.10.5 They were editors, program officers, and referees, the institutional names the non-elite mathematician's fate already flowed through. (¶59)
            - 6.11 The conversion rate changed: visibility now converts into grants, positions, media presence, and prominence in AI training data. (¶60)
                - 6.11.1 The incentive to be seen judging well now competes with judging well. (¶60)
                - 6.11.2 Once influence is measurable and monetizable it gets optimized for, and the target diverges from what it proxied. (¶60)
                - 6.11.3 Nuance: This is the measurement trap, applied to the field itself. (¶60)
            - 6.12 Example: The author's simulation of classical music canon formation applies almost directly. (¶61)
                - 6.12.1 Reshuffle access to the exposure pipeline and you get almost entirely different canons. (¶61)
                - 6.12.2 Quality sets a floor and a ceiling; the vast middle is a lottery. (¶61)
                - 6.12.3 Citation networks already made the pre-AI canon path-dependent, so an attention-mediated canon would be worse. (¶61)
                - 6.12.4 Selection pressure shifts from what nearby researchers found useful to what is legible, narratable, and aligned with a curator's brand. (¶61)
                - 6.12.5 It applies harder to theorems: a result nobody reads is inert, so reputation and readership are the same gate. (¶61)
                - 6.12.6 Whoever controls the gate controls what exists. (¶61)
            - 6.13 The floor is checkability: a result that fails to check does not survive, no matter who wants it to. (¶62)
                - 6.13.1 Machine-checked proofs are the clearest case of the world pushing back. (¶62)
            - 6.14 The ceiling is structural value: a result nothing can build on cannot be talked into importance. (¶62)
                - 6.14.1 The social layer decides the vast middle, still decisive because almost every career lives there. (¶62)
            - 6.15 A Goodhart recursion Tao does not reach: once attention is the metric, AI tools optimize for attention. (¶63)
                - 6.15.1 Results get chosen and framed for virality among mathematicians, proofs of statements that make good talks. (¶63)
                - 6.15.2 The pipeline is a loop: which results win attention feeds back into which problems are posed next. (¶63)
                - 6.15.3 So the attention economy reaches all the way back to problem selection. (¶63)
            - 6.16 The community's last non-automatable function becomes the new proxy target. (¶64)
                - 6.16.1 If models become the primary readers and rankers, today's canon biases get frozen in and amplified. (¶64)
                - 6.16.2 The influencers of today become the training distribution of tomorrow. (¶64)
                - 6.16.3 Moradifam's stable arbiter, the collective culture, is precisely what is up for grabs. (¶64)
                - 6.16.4 Counterpoint: One might argue this is exactly the best thing for the community and the science. (¶64) [counter]
                    - 6.16.4.1 Rebuttal: Judging from how social media turned out, the author highly doubts it. (¶64)
            - 6.17 It is not all doom: Polymath-style collaboration, bloggers posting proofs in progress, and arXiv culture expanded who could participate in judgment. (¶65)
                - 6.17.1 The failure mode is not visibility but a selection mechanism shifting from trusted by peers to performing on engagement metrics. (¶65)
                - 6.17.2 Mathematics was partly protected because its audience is small and expert: engagement farming fails when readers can check your proofs. (¶65)
                - 6.17.3 The danger with AI is that it changes the audience. (¶65)
        - 7 The craft's middle can empty out, as woodworking shows, taking with it the people who make results legible, checkable, and taught. (¶66, ¶67, ¶70)
            - 7.1 Example: Woodworking went through this last century: CNC machines and high-throughput manufacturing changed furniture. (¶67)
                - 7.1.1 Most furniture is now the Ikea kind: mass produced, cheap, replaceable. (¶67)
            - 7.2 A good chunk is still built at scale, but smaller than Ikea, with more variety and local taste; custom pieces still cost thousands of dollars. (¶68)
                - 7.2.1 Woodworking did not disappear; some things even cost less made by humans than bought. (¶68)
                - 7.2.2 In volume we get the simplest functional things, with little room left for creativity. (¶68)
                - 7.2.3 Not every neighborhood has a carpenter building for its people, and the creative carpenter cannot make a living. (¶68)
                - 7.2.4 At best they fix Ikea furniture, but it is so cheap nobody fixes it; they buy a new one. (¶68)
            - 7.3 Example: AI software shows the same shape: an app generated in minutes, the real work discovered in the details, then abandonment. (¶69)
                - 7.3.1 The volume of starts goes up, the number of finishes goes down, and the middle, where the craft lives, is the desert. (¶69)
            - 7.4 That middle is the digestion bottleneck Tao was trying to protect: exposition, verification, canonicalization, first to empty out. (¶70)
                - 7.4.1 If it empties like furniture's middle, mathematics keeps producing results while losing the people who make them legible, checkable, and taught. (¶70)
        - 8 Agreement is not evidence: shared conclusions without shared reasoning are hollow, and the attention economy manufactures exactly that. (¶71, ¶74, ¶77)
            - 8.1 The desert in the middle is also about who you can trust to tell you what a result means. (¶72)
                - 8.1.1 Nuance: The author says the epistemology is the part that matters most. (¶72)
            - 8.2 When someone agrees, the author asks why; sometimes the different reasoning gives a new perspective, sometimes he disregards the agreement entirely. (¶73)
                - 8.2.1 The decision was never the end goal, the way it is sometimes portrayed; the process of reaching it was. (¶73)
            - 8.3 The default social epistemology is conclusion-matching: you say X, I believe X, we are allies, and your belief strengthens mine. (¶74)
                - 8.3.1 Two people at the same conclusion via different reasoning are not in the same epistemic state; the interesting information lives in the delta. (¶74)
                - 8.3.2 If their path is sound and different, you now have two independent lines of support: genuine corroboration. (¶74)
                - 8.3.3 If their path is broken, their agreement is evidence against your confidence in a strange way: the conclusion is reachable by bad reasoning. (¶74)
                - 8.3.4 It also means your own arrival needs at least a recheck, and the person sharing your conclusion is not necessarily your ally. (¶74)
                - 8.3.5 Agreement without shared reasoning is coincidence wearing the costume of confirmation. (¶74)
            - 8.4 This breaks in influencer dynamics: deference to a Tao-tier figure is conclusion-adoption with the reasoning step amputated. (¶75)
                - 8.4.1 You inherit the belief without the justification and cannot detect when it would not survive your own scrutiny. (¶75)
                - 8.4.2 Scaled up, a community's beliefs correlate because everyone copied from the same node, not because reasoning converged. (¶75)
            - 8.5 It looks like consensus, which we treat as strong evidence, but the effective sample size is one. (¶76)
                - 8.5.1 Evidence: Atiyah's last claim about proving the Riemann Hypothesis had all the authority yet did not stand up under harsh scrutiny. (¶76)
                - 8.5.2 Nuance: One might say he wasn't even wrong. (¶76)
                - 8.5.3 Imagine a million of these every day, on far less important topics, stacked into the body of AI-assisted mathematics. (¶76)
            - 8.6 The attention economy manufactures hollow agreement: it propagates conclusions at high velocity while reasoning gets stripped in transit. (¶77)
            - 8.7 An AI answer is the limit case of conclusion without process. (¶78)
                - 8.7.1 The fluent AI explanation is engineered to feel like the perspective-gaining moment without necessarily being one. (¶78)
                - 8.7.2 Nuance: The danger is not only students confusing exposure with ownership, though that is real. (¶78)
                - 8.7.3 It is that everyone loses the practice of treating why as the real question and the conclusion as merely its byproduct. (¶78)
            - 8.8 Nuance: The author connects this to the tree falling in a jungle thought experiment. (¶79)
        - 9 Correctness was only the floor: importance is the real constraint, and the deciding was never paid work with a home in the profession. (¶80, ¶81, ¶85)
            - 9.1 The constraint was always importance: deciding what is worth attention, what is deep, what should be taught to the next generation. (¶81)
                - 9.1.1 Importance is partly social: checkability and structural value set floor and ceiling, the social layer decides the middle, shaped by careers, incentives, taste, and luck. (¶81)
                - 9.1.2 Nuance: Confession: the author, a practicing mathematician, always thought math was pure correctness and the art of finding beautiful patterns. (¶81)
            - 9.2 Many mathematicians publish anything they can prove, and much of science works that way. (¶82)
                - 9.2.1 Example: A scientist proposes a big problem, gets funded, works for years, publishes a detour, and the big problem quietly stays unsolved. (¶82)
            - 9.3 The answer is not the production of correct statements of found patterns; mathematics was always both finding and proving. (¶83)
                - 9.3.1 The proving is the chore, the checking, now cheap; the finding is deciding which patterns are worth proving, still human. (¶83)
                - 9.3.2 Nuance: The author is not claiming mathematics was always mostly the finding. (¶83)
            - 9.4 Speculation: the mix is shifting, as proving and verification get automated the weight moves to deciding which patterns matter, where the field's values will live. (¶84)
                - 9.4.1 Example: Software engineering already made this turn: engineers spend more time deciding what to build than writing code. (¶84)
                - 9.4.2 Some became product managers, some architects; the coding got cheap and the deciding got loud. (¶84)
                - 9.4.3 Did the turn land well? Both ways: open source runs on love, the framework wars run on attention, software is both futures at once. (¶84)
            - 9.5 Mathematics is heading the same way, with one difference: the deciding was never counted or paid as work, so it has no established home. (¶85)
                - 9.5.1 Nuance: It was a personal measure of someone great: how much my work is directing the field. (¶85)
                - 9.5.2 The AI era does not replace the practice; it reveals it. (¶85)
            - 9.6 When proving is automated, finding, judgment, and taste become visible as the only parts that cannot be generated on demand. (¶86)
                - 9.6.1 What is revealed has to land somewhere: either a fun activity for those who can afford it, or an influencer economy. (¶86)
                - 9.6.2 Then the deciding gets made by whoever holds attention, or whoever owns the compute it runs on. (¶86)
            - 9.7 The community can try to keep hold of the deciding, but it was never fully theirs: physics and usefulness steered it from outside. (¶87)
                - 9.7.1 That steering at least answered to results, while what replaces it answers to engagement. (¶87)
                - 9.7.2 The author does not know which way it lands; that is the point: it is not decided yet. (¶87)
            - 9.8 Statistics landed well when arithmetic got cheap: calculation became a tool and the field shifted from computing to designing the questions. (¶88)
                - 9.8.1 Deciding which questions matter became the discipline itself. (¶88)
                - 9.8.2 Prestige is assignable: the community made a man famous for exposition, so the middle can be revalued if it wants to be. (¶88)
            - 9.9 The woodworking story is the same story told in furniture: the craft does not die, it stops being how people make a living. (¶89)
                - 9.9.1 The question is whether the craft still has a middle, whether the creative carpenter can still be paid for being creative. (¶89)
            - 9.10 Thinking is what we are for; if we outsource it, there is little else we are good for. (¶90)
                - 9.10.1 Mathematics has been for centuries one of the main places we practice thinking in public, with the stakes visible and the errors checkable. (¶90)
            - 9.11 The question was never whether machines can do math; they can, and they will do more of it. (¶91)
                - 9.11.1 The question is whether we keep doing the point: framing the problem, judging what matters, and actually coming to understand. (¶91)
                - 9.11.2 The struggle is the product. (¶91)
            - 9.12 The proof is the gym: lifting weights is not the sport and barely resembles it, and almost nobody lifts for fun. (¶92)
                - 9.12.1 The athlete who stops lifting cannot perform at that level: that is the difference between the coach and the pro athlete. (¶92)
                - 9.12.2 The coach has the ideas, taste, and judgment, can tell you what to do and why, but cannot do it anymore, maybe never could. (¶92)
                - 9.12.3 Automating proving does not merely cheapen a chore: it risks stopping the training that produces people who can find patterns. (¶92)
                - 9.12.4 Imagine the athletes are robots with extreme powers: the coach still decides which patterns to chase, but the gym belongs to the robot. (¶92)
                - 9.12.5 A coach who only ever coaches robots keeps the taste and loses the performance; judgment floats free of any act of doing. (¶92)
